<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Young</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a>–<a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Young was a British polymath, born in 1773 into a Quaker family of Milverton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, who made contributions to the study of vision, light, solid mechanics, energy, physiology, language, musical harmony and Egyptology. A physician by training, he lectured at the Royal Institution from 1801. With the ripple tank and a predecessor of the double-slit experiment he demonstrated interference, supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-huygens">Christiaan Huygens</a>' wave theory of light against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>'s view of light as particles. He described Young's modulus in 1807, and from 1813 worked on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a>, where several scholars see his true contribution in the decipherment of the demotic script.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-young-scientist.jpg" alt="Thomas Young" width="330" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Perronet Briggs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Young_by_Briggs_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The English scholar <b>Thomas Young</b> made progress with demotic and the royal names, and in 1822 Jean-François Champollion, who knew Coptic well, showed that hieroglyphs combined signs for sounds and for meanings, reading the names of Ptolemy and Cleopatra and then of the pharaohs Ramesses and Thutmose.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li></ul></section>
